    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it, so it's basically just biological math. but sometimes it feels like no matter how much we adjust the program, it's like there's this invisible wall they hit. it's almost frustrating how random it feels when the science says they should be improving but they just stall out. how do you know for sure if it's actual fatigue or if the program just isn't working anymore?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between fatigue and a program failure, observe if strength drops below 90% of their previous peak during a specific set or if technique breaks down due to specific movement faults rather than just general exhaustion. True fatigue usually resolves completely within a standard 3-7 day rest period without losing significant relative strength, whereas a non-responding program often requires a complete restructuring of variables like range of motion progression or exercise selection. You can also track the rate of strength gain over time; a flat trendline despite equal effort often signals the program structure has become counterproductive or too advanced. Ultimately, listening to how the body responds during the off-week is the best indicator of whether the issue is purely systemic fatigue or a deeper mismatch in training stimuli.",
      "source": "target",
